       <h2 id="s4"><span class="section-number">4</span>Two repairs, and what they guarantee</h2>
 
-      <p><strong>Method 1, clip and rescale.</strong> Given a real symmetric \(A\), compute \(B = A_0\) by Definition 3.1, then \(R = D^{-1/2} B D^{-1/2}\) by Proposition 3.4, when every diagonal entry of \(B\) is positive. Under that positive-diagonal condition, Propositions 3.2(a) and 3.4 prove that \(R\) is a correlation matrix. Proposition 3.3 gives the exact squared distance \(\lVert A - B \rVert_F^{2}\) of the first step. It does not give \(\lVert A - R \rVert_F\), because the rescaling moves the matrix again, and the lesson proves no formula for that distance.</p>
+      <p><strong>Method 1, clip and rescale.</strong> Given a real symmetric \(A\), compute \(B = A_0\) by Definition 3.1, then \(R = D^{-1/2} B D^{-1/2}\) by Proposition 3.4, when every diagonal entry of \(B\) is positive. Under that positive-diagonal condition, Propositions 3.2(a) and 3.4 prove that \(R\) is a correlation matrix. Proposition 3.3 gives the exact squared distance \(\lVert A - B \rVert_F^{2}\) of the first step. It does not give \(\lVert A - R \rVert_F\), because the rescaling moves the matrix again, and this lesson gives no general formula for that distance. Example 3.6 computes it for this one matrix only.</p>
 
       <p><strong>Method 2, alternating projections.</strong> Start from \(X = A\). Repeat: clip \(X\) at zero to get \(Y\), set every diagonal entry of \(Y\) to \(1\), and stop when the largest entrywise change from \(X\) to \(Y\) is below \(10^{-12}\) or after 500 steps. The output \(W\) has unit diagonal by construction. The construction does not guarantee that \(W\) is PSD, and on the seeded inputs the lab checks it only to within \(10^{-8}\). The lesson does not prove that the procedure converges, or that it reaches a nearest correlation matrix.</p>
 
